import{at as e,it as t,n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.UwwvkjGo.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Data-driven architecture","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Data-driven architecture","group":"Documents","category":"Guides","head":[["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Data-driven architecture"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":""}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://jsprismarine.github.io/JSPrismarine/data-driven/architecture"}],["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://jsprismarine.github.io/JSPrismarine/data-driven/architecture"}]],"editSource":{"kind":"file","url":"https://github.com/JSPrismarine/JSPrismarine/edit/master/docs/data-driven/architecture.md"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"data-driven/architecture.md","filePath":"data-driven/architecture.md"}`),a={name:`data-driven/architecture.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<div><h1 id="data-driven-architecture" tabindex="-1">Data-driven architecture <a class="header-anchor" href="#data-driven-architecture" aria-label="Permalink to “Data-driven architecture”">​</a></h1><p>This page explains how the data-driven system is put together: what a pack stack is, what loading one produces, how definitions become running content, why a single module owns the Java to Bedrock translation, and which parts of this exist in the repository today. The design source is <a href="/JSPrismarine/plans/data-driven-vanilla.html">the data-driven vanilla plan</a>, sections 5 and 7.</p><h2 id="the-pack-stack" tabindex="-1">The pack stack <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-pack-stack" aria-label="Permalink to “The pack stack”">​</a></h2><p>A pack stack is the ordered list of content a world runs, and it holds two kinds of member. Bedrock packs (the vanilla behavior pack as BDS ships it, this project&#39;s overlay, world packs, plugin packs) define content: blocks, items, entities, biomes, spawn rules, trade tables. Java datapacks (the vanilla datapack from Mojang&#39;s data generator, world datapacks, plugin datapacks) configure that content: loot tables, recipes, advancements, tags, enchantments, damage types, predicates and item modifiers.</p><p>The split follows what each format can express. A Java datapack almost never defines a block, item or entity; it references vanilla ones. A Bedrock pack is the only format of the two that can define content a Bedrock client renders, because a Bedrock client learns about custom content exclusively from a downloaded resource pack and NBT in <code>StartGamePacket</code> and <code>ItemRegistryPacket</code>.</p><p><code>@jsprismarine/packs</code> (in <code>packages/packs/</code>) owns loading. Its <code>load(stack)</code> function takes the ordered list and returns a <code>Definitions</code> object, one map from identifier to winning parsed definition per registry, plus a diagnostics report. Each format keeps its own precedence rules within its half: Bedrock registries resolve by identifier with a later pack replacing an earlier definition whole, while Java registries resolve per file, except tags, whose values merge across packs unless a later file sets <code>replace</code>. <a href="/JSPrismarine/data-driven/using-packs.html">using-packs.md</a> covers the loader in detail.</p><h2 id="definitions-types-and-instances" tabindex="-1">Definitions, types and instances <a class="header-anchor" href="#definitions-types-and-instances" aria-label="Permalink to “Definitions, types and instances”">​</a></h2><p>The runtime distinguishes three stages of the same content.</p><p>A definition is parsed, validated, immutable JSON for one identifier, exactly as the winning pack provided it. A type is a definition resolved against the component registries once at load time, with behavior hooks bound and frequently checked flags computed. An instance is the live thing in the world: a block state in a chunk, an item stack, or an entity.</p><p>Resolution flattens the values the server checks on every tick into typed arrays indexed by runtime id: solidity, light emission and filtering, blast resistance, friction, random tick, support and liquid rules. The values in those arrays come from the Java jar dump rather than Bedrock&#39;s component grammar, because the grammar cannot hold Java&#39;s values. Bedrock&#39;s <code>minecraft:friction</code> component is documented as capping at 0.9 while Java&#39;s blue ice has friction 0.989, so the table is this project&#39;s own shape. <a href="/JSPrismarine/data-driven/block-properties.html">block-properties.md</a> describes the table.</p><h2 id="components-and-behaviors" tabindex="-1">Components and behaviors <a class="header-anchor" href="#components-and-behaviors" aria-label="Permalink to “Components and behaviors”">​</a></h2><p>A component is one documented component name bound to a TypeScript class with a parameter schema and hooks. Each domain (blocks, items, entities) has a registry the server fills with the vanilla components and plugins extend with their own.</p><p>Behavior that data cannot express binds by family in this project&#39;s own overlay namespace. Mojang&#39;s <code>minecraft:custom_components</code> hook is read as one more way to name a behavior rather than the binding mechanism, because Mojang has deprecated version 1 of that hook and version 2 requires two experimental toggles. For entities, the component list is the definition&#39;s <code>components</code>, with <code>component_groups</code> and <code>events</code> acting as the state machine, and AI goals as components with a priority and control flags.</p><h2 id="the-java-to-bedrock-seam" tabindex="-1">The Java to Bedrock seam <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-java-to-bedrock-seam" aria-label="Permalink to “The Java to Bedrock seam”">​</a></h2><p>One module, <code>@jsprismarine/mapping</code> (in <code>packages/mapping/</code>), owns the knowledge that two editions exist. Nothing else in the server is allowed to name both.</p><p>The seam exists because the two halves of the format decision meet in the middle: Java-shaped data (a loot table dropping <code>minecraft:oak_log</code> with Java state <code>axis=x</code>) has to land on Bedrock-shaped registries (<code>minecraft:oak_log</code> with Bedrock state <code>pillar_axis=x</code>). The translation is one-way, Java into Bedrock, and the asymmetry is the reason it is tractable. Java data references content; Bedrock packs define it. References need a lookup table, definitions need none, and a server never performs the reverse trip, so the 11000 hand-written lines a bidirectional model like Chunker&#39;s costs are avoided entirely. The research measured the fit: all 35723 Java 26.3 block states resolve to a real entry in Bedrock&#39;s 22091-state palette, and exactly 5 Java states have no Bedrock concept.</p><p>The interface is four lookup functions (block state, item, entity type, and identifier per registry), each returning a typed result rather than <code>undefined</code>: <code>mapped</code>, a reviewed <code>loss</code> with a reason and a reference, <code>passthrough</code>, or <code>unknown</code>. The tables behind them are generated from the pinned GeyserMC/mappings commit and committed to the repository, so a version bump regenerates them and the diff is reviewable. Three rules keep the seam honest: the loader refuses a Java identifier with neither a mapping nor a loss entry, every loss is a reviewed row with a reason, and the tables are generated rather than hand-maintained. <a href="/JSPrismarine/data-driven/mapping.html">mapping.md</a> covers the package.</p><h2 id="registries" tabindex="-1">Registries <a class="header-anchor" href="#registries" aria-label="Permalink to “Registries”">​</a></h2><p>Each domain gets one registry shape holding the resolved types, with tags merged across the stack. The client-facing packets (<code>ItemRegistryPacket</code>, the block properties NBT in <code>StartGamePacket</code>, <code>CreativeContentPacket</code>) are written from the registries, which is what will give plugins custom blocks and items on the wire. Storage stays Bedrock-shaped on disk, so existing worlds remain readable throughout the migration.</p><h2 id="where-each-subsystem-lives" tabindex="-1">Where each subsystem lives <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-each-subsystem-lives" aria-label="Permalink to “Where each subsystem lives”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Subsystem</th><th>Location</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pinned sources, fetch, Java dump, captured goldens, generated data</td><td><code>packages/bedrock-data/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Pack stack loading, both formats</td><td><code>packages/packs/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Java to Bedrock mapping tables and the block property table</td><td><code>packages/mapping/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Molang parsing, compilation and evaluation</td><td><code>packages/molang/</code></td></tr><tr><td>The registry and holder types every domain is built on</td><td><code>packages/content/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Blocks and items, with the behavior families bound to them</td><td><code>packages/blocks/</code>, <code>packages/items/</code>, <code>packages/block-behaviors/</code>, <code>packages/item-behaviors/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Entities, their goals, the Java brains, spawn rules and trading</td><td><code>packages/entities/</code>, <code>packages/entity-goals/</code>, <code>packages/entity-brains/</code>, <code>packages/spawning/</code>, <code>packages/trading/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Java&#39;s gameplay data: loot, recipes, advancements, enchantments, predicates</td><td><code>packages/loot/</code>, <code>packages/recipes/</code>, <code>packages/advancements/</code>, <code>packages/enchantments/</code>, <code>packages/predicates/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Commands and the <code>.mcfunction</code> runtime</td><td><code>packages/commands/</code>, <code>packages/mcfunction/</code></td></tr><tr><td>The systems neither edition publishes as data, and light</td><td><code>packages/engine-systems/</code>, <code>packages/lighting/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Terrain, decoration and structures</td><td><code>packages/worldgen/</code>, <code>packages/features/</code>, <code>packages/structures/</code></td></tr><tr><td>The registries turned into client packet payloads</td><td><code>packages/protocol/src/payload/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Parity test suite and dashboard</td><td><code>packages/parity/</code></td></tr><tr><td>The composition root that joins all of it to a running server</td><td><code>packages/prismarine/src/content/</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="what-exists-today" tabindex="-1">What exists today <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-exists-today" aria-label="Permalink to “What exists today”">​</a></h2><p>The pack stack is no longer optional. Every server composes one, resolves each content domain against it and runs on the result, and a stack that will not load stops the server at startup because nothing is left behind it to fall back to. The composition root in <code>packages/prismarine/src/content/</code> is the only place that knows all the domains exist; its <a href="https://github.com/JSPrismarine/JSPrismarine/blob/master/packages/prismarine/src/content/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">README</a> gives the order they have to be built in and what one build produces.</p><p>Blocks, items, entities, recipes, loot, advancements, enchantments, spawn rules, trade tables, biomes and features all come out of the stack, and the client-facing packets are written from the resulting registries. The old mob and projectile class hierarchies are gone. Hand-written actor classes cover players and dropped items; saved projectile actors load as generic bodies and run through their <code>minecraft:projectile</code> definitions. TNT and falling blocks run from their vanilla entity definitions.</p><p>The numbers and the gaps live on the page for each subsystem rather than here, because that is where they are kept current: <a href="/JSPrismarine/data-driven/gameplay-data.html">gameplay-data.md</a> for the Java data, <a href="/JSPrismarine/data-driven/entities.html">entities.md</a> for mobs, <a href="/JSPrismarine/data-driven/block-properties.html">block-properties.md</a> for the per-state table, <a href="/JSPrismarine/data-driven/structures.html">structures.md</a> and <a href="/JSPrismarine/data-driven/engine-systems.html">engine-systems.md</a> for the world, and <a href="/JSPrismarine/data-driven/commands.html">commands.md</a> for the command set. Each of them says what it will not do yet.</p></div>`,1)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};